    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mongol khanate founded by Hulagu in Persia and Iraq after he destroyed the Abbasid Caliphate at Baghdad in 1258.</li><li>Under Ghazan it became a Persianised Islamic state whose reforms, trade and court scholarship linked China and the Mediterranean.</li><li>When Abu Said died without an heir in 1335 it broke into rival successor states such as the Jalayirids and Chupanids.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Rashid al-Din's Jami al-Tawarikh, written at the Ilkhanid court with Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants, is often called the first universal history of Eurasia.</p></aside>
    </section>

